Your `ReportGenerator` has several untestable statics. Here's the best approach for each:

## File System (`File.*`, `Directory.*`) → **System.IO.Abstractions** NuGet

This is the recommended approach — a battle-tested library, no custom wrappers needed.

**1. Install the package:**
```
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

**2. Inject `IFileSystem` into your class:**
```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        // ...
        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);

        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, content);
        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

**3. Register in DI:**
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**
```
dotnet add <TestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```
```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem();
mockFs.Directory.CreateDirectory("/tmp/reports");
var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);

generator.GenerateReport("Sales");

// Assert the file was written
var files = mockFs.Directory.GetFiles("/tmp/reports");
Assert.Single(files);
```

---

## Bonus: Other statics in `ReportGenerator`

Your class also has `DateTime.UtcNow`, `Environment.*`, and `Console.WriteLine`:

| Static | Best Abstraction |
|--------|-----------------|
| `DateTime.UtcNow` | **`TimeProvider`** (built-in on .NET 10) — inject it, call `timeProvider.GetUtcNow()`, test with `FakeTimeProvider` from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` |
| `Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable` / `MachineName` | Custom **`IEnvironmentProvider`** — wrap only the two methods you use |
| `Console.WriteLine` | Custom **`IConsole`** or just use `ILogger<T>` if you already have logging |

